.so man.macros
.TH ck::open n 1.0 Ck "Ck Built-In Commands"
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
ck::open \- Create the Ck main window and start a curses session
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBck::open \fR?\fIoption value ...\fR? ?\fIpath\fR?
.BE

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBck::open\fR creates the Ck main window (\fI.\fR) and starts a curses
session against it.  Successive calls to \fBck::open\fR within the same
interpreter are not permitted while a session is active; the existing
session must be torn down with \fBdestroy .\fR first.
.PP
On a successful return the path of the created window (currently
always ``\fB.\fR'') is left as the interpreter's result and the runtime
Tcl-side bindings are loaded as a side effect (the same set of files
\fB$ck_library/ck.tcl\fR sources for class bindings, dialog defaults,
and so on).
.PP
\fBck::open\fR has two operating modes:
.TP
\fBControlling-tty mode\fR (default — no \fB-pty\fR option supplied)
The session takes over the process's controlling terminal.  fds 0/1/2
are dup-saved to high-numbered slots, the saved out/in fds are wrapped
in \fBFILE\ *\fRs and handed to ncurses' \fBnewterm\fR(3) for display
output and keyboard input, and a private capture buffer (\fBmemfd_create\fR(2)
on Linux, \fBtmpfile\fR(3) elsewhere) is dup'd over fds 1/2 so that
any \fBputs\fR, \fBprintf\fR, or extension-emitted output during the
session is collected rather than corrupting the curses display.  At
session teardown (\fBdestroy .\fR or process exit), the saved fds are
restored over 1/2, the capture is drained to the now-restored stderr,
and the curses screen is freed.  This is the behaviour applications
that previously relied on the implicit \fBpackage require ck\fR
auto-open get for free.
.TP
\fBExplicit-pty mode\fR (\fB-pty\fR \fIfd\fR)
The caller supplies an open file descriptor (or Tcl channel name)
referring to one end of a pty pair; ncurses runs against \fIfd\fR
and the host process's fds 0/1/2 are left untouched.  This is the
mode test harnesses use after allocating a pair via \fBck::pty open\fR
\(em the harness reads from the master end to inspect what the curses
session drew, and writes to it to inject keystrokes.

.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.TP
\fB-pty\fR \fIfdOrChannel\fR
Drive the curses session on the given file descriptor instead of the
process's controlling terminal.  \fIfdOrChannel\fR may be either a
small non-negative integer (a raw fd, e.g. the slave end of a pty pair)
or the name of an existing Tcl channel (e.g. the slave channel returned
by \fBck::pty\ open\fR), in which case the underlying fd is extracted
via \fBTcl_GetChannelHandle\fR.
.TP
\fB-rows\fR \fIN\fR
Force the new session's row count to \fIN\fR by issuing
\fBTIOCSWINSZ\fR on the underlying pty before \fBnewterm\fR(3) reads
the terminal size.  Must be a positive integer; takes effect only
together with \fB-cols\fR.  In controlling-tty mode the value is
informational only \(em the kernel-reported tty size wins.
.TP
\fB-cols\fR \fIN\fR
Like \fB-rows\fR but for column count.  Both must be set together for
the resize to apply.
.TP
\fB-class\fR \fIclassName\fR
Override the option-database class for the new main window.  When
omitted the class is derived from the basename of \fB$argv0\fR
(capitalised), defaulting to \fBCk\fR if \fB$argv0\fR is unset.
.TP
\fB-term\fR \fItermName\fR
Override the terminal type passed to \fBnewterm\fR(3).  Defaults to
the value of \fB$env(TERM)\fR.
.TP
\fIpath\fR
Currently must be ``\fB.\fR'' if supplied (Ck only supports a single
main window per interpreter); the option is reserved for future
multi-root support.

.SH AUTOMATIC OPEN AND THE \fB::ck_no_auto_open\fR GLOBAL
.PP
Historical Ck applications relied on \fBpackage require ck\fR creating
the main window as a side effect.  That contract is preserved: the
package's \fBpkgIndex.tcl\fR calls \fBck::open .\fR after loading the
shared library unless the global variable \fB::ck_no_auto_open\fR is
set to a true value beforehand.  Setting it suppresses the implicit
open and leaves the caller responsible for invoking \fBck::open\fR
explicitly (typically with \fB-pty\fR).
.PP
A plain top-level global is used (rather than a \fB::ck::no_auto_open\fR
namespace var) so the suppression can be set without first creating
the \fB::ck\fR namespace, which doesn't exist until the package has
been loaded:
.CS
set ::ck_no_auto_open 1
package require ck
lassign [\fBck::pty open\fR \-rows 24 \-cols 80] master slave
\fBck::open\fR \-pty $slave .
.CE
The variable is consulted only at \fBpackage require\fR time; setting
it after the package has loaded has no effect on subsequent
\fBck::open\fR calls.

.SH STDOUT / STDERR CAPTURE
.PP
In controlling-tty mode, while the session is active the process's
\fBstdout\fR and \fBstderr\fR are diverted to a capture buffer.  Any
output written through them \(em including \fBputs\fR from Tcl,
\fBprintf\fR from C extensions, the default \fBbgerror\fR fallback,
and so on \(em is collected in memory rather than written to the
display.  At teardown the original fds are reattached and the
captured bytes are dumped to the now-restored \fBstderr\fR, so a
diagnostic that would have been silently lost becomes visible the
moment the curses screen tears down.
.PP
This supersedes Ck's previous strategy of intercepting the Tcl
\fBputs\fR/\fBclose\fR/\fBflush\fR/\fBread\fR/\fBgets\fR/\fBexec\fR
commands at the script level.  The fd-level capture covers C
extensions and subprocess output that the Tcl-level intercepts
couldn't reach, and it doesn't rely on any objCmd-versus-stringCmd
trampolining in Tcl 9.

.SH SEE ALSO
ck::pty(n), destroy(n), exit(n)

.SH KEYWORDS
curses, main window, pty, capture, stdout, stderr
